  "Marinade|mSOL liquid staking": {
    "audit_security": { why: "Marinade’s custom staking program has been reviewed by several independent security firms, including Kudelski, Ackee, Neodyme, and Sec3. Marinade also has SOC 2 Type I and Type II reports. Until a July 2026 upgrade, we could verify that the deployed program matched published source code.", held: "The July 2026 upgrade broke that verification. The currently deployed program is newer than the recorded audits, and Marinade does not publish a release artifact or expected hash that would let us independently verify the new binary against public source code." },
    "maturity": { why: "Marinade is the oldest liquid staking product we reviewed, operating since 2021. It has nearly five years of history, remains actively developed, and continues to operate with a live public API.", held: "mSOL’s current TVL, about $183.8M at the time of review, is substantially below its previous peak. The exact program version currently deployed is also relatively new, at roughly 25 days old when we assessed it." },
    "upgrade_admin_authority": { why: "Marinade documents a 13-party multisig requiring 6 approvals for important administrative and upgrade actions. User SOL remains controlled by the staking program, and users retain a permissionless standard withdrawal path.", held: "We could not independently verify the documented 13-party, 6-signature authority structure on-chain. The upgrade authority also exercised its power only about 25 days before our assessment." },
    "manager_discretion": { why: "Marinade’s operators cannot directly take custody of users’ staked SOL, and users retain a standard withdrawal path at the pool’s underlying value.", held: "Validator allocation is managed through Marinade’s Stake Auction Marketplace (SAM), an automated delegation system that has previously experienced a reward-impacting failure. We also verified the current 0% reward fee, but could not verify a hard limit on how much that fee could be increased in the deployed program." },
    "strategy_complexity": { why: "The core product remains straightforward liquid staking without lending, leverage, or additional DeFi strategies.", held: "Marinade uses its own custom staking program, its SAM validator-allocation system, and an internal instant-unstake liquidity pool. That creates more moving parts than liquid staking products built on the standard SPL Stake Pool program." },
    "composability_dependency": { why: "User principal primarily depends on Marinade’s staking program and Solana’s native staking system. There are no additional external DeFi protocols in the custody path.", held: "Fast withdrawals depend on Marinade’s internal liquidity pool and external market liquidity, while validator allocation and staking performance depend on the SAM system." },
    "oracle_risk": { why: "Marinade does not rely on an external price oracle to determine the underlying SOL value of mSOL. The mSOL/SOL exchange rate comes from staking-pool state, and we verified a rate of 1.3994 SOL per mSOL at the time of review.", held: "mSOL’s market price can trade below its underlying SOL value during periods of stress. This happened previously, including in December 2023, and our current liquidity tests show that larger exits can again create a meaningful discount." },
    "liquidity_withdrawal": { why: "Users always retain a standard withdrawal path at the pool’s underlying value, although it requires waiting through the unstaking process. Marinade also provides an instant-unstake option with a dynamic fee ranging from 0.1% to 9%, depending on available liquidity.", held: "Market liquidity weakened significantly at larger trade sizes in our tests. A 10,000 mSOL exit was about 0.64% below underlying value, a 25,000 mSOL exit was about 2.3% below, and our 50,000 mSOL test found no viable market route." },
    "asset_stablecoin_risk": { why: "mSOL is backed by natively staked SOL rather than stablecoins, a collateral basket, or another DeFi asset. Users who can wait for the normal unstaking process can redeem based on the pool’s underlying SOL value.", held: "mSOL can temporarily trade below that underlying value. The December 2023 mSOL dislocation demonstrates that this risk is real, even though users can avoid accepting the market discount by waiting for the standard withdrawal process." },
    "withdrawal_fee_transparency": { why: "Marinade’s fee disclosures matched what we could verify. The documented 0% fee on staking rewards is confirmed on-chain, and the documented 0.2% direct-unstake fee and dynamic instant-unstake fee were consistent with the product’s published information.", held: "The instant-unstake fee can vary substantially, from 0.1% to 9% depending on liquidity. This is disclosed, but users need to understand that the cost of an immediate exit can become significant during stressed conditions." },
    "operational_dependency_risk": { why: "Marinade distributes stake across multiple validators rather than concentrating it with one validator, and the protocol has a long operating history.", held: "Validator allocation depends on Marinade’s SAM automation, which is the same system involved in the previously recorded delegation failure. We also found limited information about the operational controls and safeguards around that system." },
    "incident_history": { why: "The known incident did not result in loss of user principal, and Marinade corrected the underlying problem.", held: "A bug in the SAM delegation system caused approximately 37,000 SOL in missed staking rewards across multiple epochs. The issue was disclosed through a community forum, but we could not find a formal Marinade postmortem explaining the cause, impact, and remediation in detail." },
  },
  // HAND-AUTHORED, PRODUCT-OWNER-APPROVED user-facing copy (2026-08-11).
