  "Meteora|Liquidity pools": {
    "audit_security": { why: "Meteora has a substantial security-review program, with audits from several independent firms across multiple releases and an active bug bounty. The audit history suggests the deployed version has received meaningful review.", held: "We could not verify that the exact DLMM program currently running on-chain matches a specific audited release. Meteora does not publish a verified build or expected program hash that would let us independently confirm that link." },
    "maturity": { why: "Meteora’s team has roots going back to Mercurial in 2021, while the current Meteora product family has operated since around 2023. At the time of review, the protocol had about $271M in TVL and had handled several periods of very high Solana trading activity without a documented smart-contract failure.", held: "DLMM itself has a shorter track record of roughly 2.5–3 years, and the exact program version we reviewed had been deployed for only about 68 days." },
    "upgrade_admin_authority": { why: "User liquidity positions are controlled by the program, and users can withdraw their positions without requiring approval from Meteora.", held: "Meteora’s DLMM program can be upgraded, but we could not find public information explaining who controls the upgrade authority, whether it is a multisig, or what approval process is required." },
    "manager_discretion": { why: "Meteora cannot directly move or seize a user’s individual LP position. Users retain control over withdrawing their liquidity.", held: "Some Meteora products automatically move unused liquidity into Dynamic Vaults, which can lend those assets to external protocols. This means part of an LP position can be deployed elsewhere without the user selecting those lending protocols directly." },
    "strategy_complexity": { why: "Meteora’s architecture is public and has received security review across multiple components.", held: "It is also the most complex product structure we reviewed. The broader Meteora product family includes multiple interacting programs, while DLMM itself uses concentrated liquidity bins, dynamic fees, and, in some products, an additional lending-vault layer. More moving parts create more places where something can go wrong." },
    "composability_dependency": { why: "Liquidity deposited directly into a DLMM pool remains within that pool’s core program rather than being routed through multiple DeFi protocols by default.", held: "Dynamic AMM products can move idle liquidity into external lending protocols through Dynamic Vaults. That means users may indirectly take on the liquidity and operational risks of another protocol." },
    "oracle_risk": { why: "Meteora does not depend on an external price oracle for its core AMM pricing. The pool itself determines the trading price, so there is no external price feed that can become stale.", held: "AMM prices can still be temporarily distorted by large trades, pool imbalances, or transaction ordering. Dynamic fees help reduce this risk, but they do not remove it." },
    "liquidity_withdrawal": { why: "Users can generally withdraw their own LP positions directly and without a fixed waiting period, withdrawal queue, or epoch-based delay.", held: "If part of the liquidity has been deployed through a Dynamic Vault into an external lending protocol, that portion may depend on liquidity being available in the underlying lending market at the time of withdrawal." },
    "asset_stablecoin_risk": { why: "The assets held by each liquidity position are transparent and determined by the selected trading pair.", held: "Liquidity providers face **impermanent loss by design**. When the prices of the two assets move relative to each other, an LP position can become worth less than simply holding the same assets. This risk exists even when both assets and the protocol function exactly as intended." },
    "withdrawal_fee_transparency": { why: "Meteora’s pool fees are visible on-chain and surfaced in its product interface. We did not find a mismatch between the fee information shown to users and the deployed configuration.", held: "Fees differ by pool and can change dynamically with market conditions, so there is no single protocol-wide fee that applies to every Meteora position." },
    "operational_dependency_risk": { why: "Meteora’s pools operate permissionlessly, so users are not dependent on an operator manually processing normal trades or withdrawals.", held: "We found unresolved concerns around the organization itself, including leadership changes, a compromised communications account, and ongoing litigation. The lack of transparency around the program upgrade authority adds to that operational uncertainty." },
    "incident_history": { why: "We found no documented smart-contract exploit resulting in loss of funds from Meteora’s core protocol.", held: "Meteora and related parties are defendants in an ongoing class-action lawsuit connected to the LIBRA and MELANIA token events. The lawsuit alleges that Meteora infrastructure was used as part of coordinated liquidity schemes. These are allegations, not findings of wrongdoing, but they remain unresolved. Meteora also announced an independent investigation in February 2025, and we could not find a published final report." },
  },
  // HAND-AUTHORED, PRODUCT-OWNER-APPROVED user-facing copy (2026-08-11).
