  "Orca|Liquidity pools": {
    "audit_security": { why: "Orca has one of the strongest audit records among the protocols we reviewed, with six audits across four independent firms. Its program is open source, and we verified that the deployed program matches the published source code.", held: "The latest audit predates the exact program version currently deployed. The changes since that audit are visible in the public repository, but they have not been independently audited." },
    "maturity": { why: "Orca has operated since 2021, and its Whirlpools product has more than four years of live history. It also remained a major Solana trading venue at the time of our review, with about $116.5M in daily volume.", held: "Current TVL, about $239M at the time of review, is below previous market-cycle peaks. The exact deployed program version is also newer, at roughly six months old." },
    "upgrade_admin_authority": { why: "The deployed program has remained stable for about six months, and Orca’s verified-build process means changes to the live program can be compared directly with public source code. Users also retain ownership of their individual LP positions.", held: "We could not find public documentation explaining who controls Orca’s program upgrade authority, whether it is a multisig, or what approval process is required." },
    "manager_discretion": { why: "Orca cannot directly move, lend, or seize a user’s LP position. There is also no additional vault layer that automatically lends or redeploys idle liquidity.", held: "Pool and fee parameters are still set at the protocol level. Changes to those parameters can affect the economics of LP positions within the limits of the program." },
    "strategy_complexity": { why: "Orca’s core architecture is comparatively contained, with a single open-source Whirlpools program rather than a large set of interacting protocols.", held: "Concentrated liquidity itself is complex. Users choose price ranges, positions can move out of range, and returns depend on range management, fee generation, and price movement. Passive positions may not perform as intended without active management." },
    "composability_dependency": { why: "LP principal primarily depends on Orca’s Whirlpools program and the standard SPL Token program. There is no external lending protocol or separate vault layer in the custody path.", held: "Jupiter and other aggregators are important for how traders interact with Orca liquidity in practice, although those services do not custody LP funds." },
    "oracle_risk": { why: "Orca does not rely on an external price oracle for its core AMM pricing. The pool itself determines the trading price, so there is no external feed that can become stale.", held: "Like other AMMs, pool prices can be temporarily affected by large trades, imbalances, or transaction ordering. This is inherent to the model rather than an oracle-specific failure." },
    "liquidity_withdrawal": { why: "Orca provides one of the cleanest withdrawal structures we reviewed. Users can withdraw their own LP positions directly and without a fixed waiting period, withdrawal queue, operator approval, or dependence on an external lending market.", held: "A withdrawal returns the assets currently held by the position at the pool’s current ratio. The exit is immediate, but the value and asset mix are not guaranteed to match what the user originally deposited." },
    "asset_stablecoin_risk": { why: "The assets held by each Orca position are transparent and determined by the selected trading pair.", held: "Concentrated liquidity carries impermanent loss by design. When relative prices move, the position can become worth less than simply holding the two assets, and concentrating liquidity into a narrower price range can amplify that effect." },
    "withdrawal_fee_transparency": { why: "Pool fees and Orca’s protocol take are visible on-chain and surfaced in its interface and documentation. We found no contradiction between the disclosed fees and the deployed configuration.", held: "Fees differ by pool and configuration, so there is no single protocol-wide fee that applies to every Orca position." },
    "operational_dependency_risk": { why: "Orca has a long operating history, a stable product, and strong verified-build practices. We found no major operational event that suggested persistent problems with the protocol.", held: "We could not find enough public detail about internal operational controls such as upgrade-key custody and deployment procedures." },
    "incident_history": { why: "We found no publicly documented smart-contract exploit, user-fund loss event, or major litigation involving Orca across more than four years of operation.", held: "This reflects the public information we could find at the time of review, not a formal or exhaustive incident-history report. We therefore do not treat the absence of discovered incidents as proof of a completely clean record." },
  },
  // HAND-AUTHORED, PRODUCT-OWNER-APPROVED user-facing copy (2026-08-11).
