  "Raydium|Liquidity pools": {
    "audit_security": { why: "Raydium’s programs are open source and have been reviewed by independent security firms, including OtterSec. The CLMM program is also linked to Raydium’s public repository.", held: "We could not verify that the exact CLMM program currently running on-chain matches an audited version. It had been upgraded only about 11 days before our assessment, and its build verification was still incomplete. The June 2026 exploit also showed that a validation flaw remained in an older part of the Raydium program family." },
    "maturity": { why: "Raydium has operated since 2021 and was the largest liquidity-pool venue we reviewed, with about $843M in TVL and roughly $84M in daily trading volume at the time of assessment. It has continued operating through multiple market cycles and two major security incidents.", held: "That long history includes two confirmed exploits resulting in user losses, in 2022 and 2026. The exact CLMM program version we reviewed was also only about 11 days old." },
    "upgrade_admin_authority": { why: "After the 2022 exploit, Raydium documented changes to strengthen control of its administrative authority, including moving key custody to a hardware wallet and later to a multisig.", held: "The 2022 exploit itself resulted from compromise of an administrative key. We could not independently verify the current control structure of Raydium’s upgrade authority, including who controls it or what approvals are required." },
    "manager_discretion": { why: "Users retain control of their LP positions, and Raydium does not automatically lend or redeploy those positions into external protocols. Raydium also reduced some administrative powers after the 2022 incident.", held: "The 2022 exploit demonstrated that administrative functions could previously affect pool funds through fee withdrawals and parameter changes. Those paths were patched, but we did not independently verify the exact administrative limits in the newly deployed program version." },
    "strategy_complexity": { why: "Raydium’s current AMM products are well established and their basic mechanics are transparent.", held: "Raydium has several generations of AMM programs still present on-chain, including legacy Serum-era pools, V4, CPMM, and CLMM. The June 2026 exploit demonstrated the risk of this accumulated complexity: pools considered retired still contained funds and remained exploitable years later." },
    "composability_dependency": { why: "Raydium’s modern CPMM and CLMM products have relatively contained custody paths, primarily depending on their own pool programs and the SPL Token program.", held: "Older Raydium pools were also dependent on external Serum/OpenBook order-book markets. Although newer products removed this dependency, the continued existence of legacy programs means that historical dependencies remain relevant to the broader Raydium system." },
    "oracle_risk": { why: "Raydium does not rely on an external price oracle for its core AMM pricing. The pool itself determines the trading price, so there is no external price feed that can become stale.", held: "Like other AMMs, prices can still be temporarily distorted through large trades, pool imbalances, or transaction ordering. This is inherent to the AMM model rather than an oracle-specific weakness." },
    "liquidity_withdrawal": { why: "Users can withdraw their own LP positions directly and without a fixed waiting period, withdrawal queue, operator approval, or dependence on an external lending protocol. Raydium also had the deepest overall liquidity among the LP protocols we reviewed.", held: "A withdrawal returns the assets currently held by the position at the pool’s current ratio. The exit is immediate, but neither the asset mix nor the position’s value is guaranteed to match what the user originally deposited." },
    "asset_stablecoin_risk": { why: "The assets held by each LP position are transparent and determined by the selected trading pair.", held: "LP positions carry **impermanent loss by design**. Their value can fall behind simply holding the underlying assets when relative prices move, and concentrated-liquidity positions can amplify that effect." },
    "withdrawal_fee_transparency": { why: "Raydium’s pool fees and protocol take are visible on-chain and surfaced in its documentation and interface. We found no contradiction between the disclosed fees and the deployed configuration.", held: "Fees differ by pool and configuration, so there is no single protocol-wide fee that applies to every Raydium position." },
    "operational_dependency_risk": { why: "Raydium has demonstrated a strong ability to respond after incidents. Its 2022 response included a same-day patch, detailed disclosure, and compensation for affected users.", held: "Two significant operational failures are now part of the record. The 2022 exploit involved an administrative key stored on a compromised machine, while the 2026 exploit involved retired programs that had not been fully decommissioned despite still holding user funds." },
    "incident_history": { why: "Raydium has been transparent about the 2022 exploit, publishing a detailed postmortem and providing compensation to affected users. The protocol also committed to refunding users affected by the June 2026 incident.", held: "Raydium is the only protocol we reviewed with two confirmed exploits that resulted in loss of user principal: approximately $5.5M in December 2022 and about $1.34M reported in June 2026. Unlike the 2022 event, we could not find an official postmortem for the June 2026 exploit at the time of our review." },
  },
